research/audit/wave12-dispatch/certifier-a8-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.log:14353:[L7] The mean value theorem: for $\psi$ continuous on $[a,b]$ with $a<b$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$ there is $\xi \in (a,b)$ with $\psi(b)-\psi(a) = \psi'(\xi)(b-a)$ ([[cor-mean-value-theorem]]).
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/certifier-a8-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.log:14395:- **$M \ge 0$ is a hypothesis, not a deduction.** It follows from $\lVert f'(t)\rVert_2 \le M$ at any single $t \in (a,b)$, and $(a,b)$ is nonempty here because $a<b$; it is stated anyway so that the conclusion reads as a genuine bound, in the style of [[cor-bounded-derivative-implies-lipschitz]].
